 "wikitext": "{{Short description|Legislative district of the Philippines}}\n{{Politics of the Philippines}}\nThe '''legislative districts of Pangasinan''' are the representations of the [[Provinces of the Philippines|province]] of [[Pangasinan]] and the [[Cities of the Philippines#Independent cities|independent component city]] of [[Dagupan]] in the [[List of legislatures of the Philippines|various national legislatures]] of the [[Philippines]]. The province and the city are currently represented in the [[House of Representatives of the Philippines|lower house]] of the [[Congress of the Philippines]] through their [[#1st District|first]], [[#2nd District|second]], [[#3rd District|third]], [[#4th District|fourth]], [[#5th District|fifth]], and [[#6th District|sixth]] congressional districts.\n\n[[File:PangasinanLegislativeDistricts2022.svg|thumb|Map of the Current Legislative districts of Pangasinan]]\n[[File:Pangasinan2009.svg|thumb|Map of the Reapportioned map of Pangasinan for House Bill No. 4140.]]\n[[File:Pangasinan2025.svg|thumb|Proposed reapportioned map of Pangasinan for House Bill No. 5311.]]\n== History == \n\nPangasinan was initially composed of one representative district, wherein it had [[#1898\u20131899|four representatives, at large]], to the [[Malolos Congress]] from 1898 to 1899. Two representatives were elected, while the other two were appointed.<ref>{{cite web |title=The Malolos Congress |url=https://repository.mainlib.upd.edu.ph/omekas/files/original/a312285dc78c8413ef7187c999619c5d71dbb34e.pdf\n |website=University of the Philippines Main Library Digital Archives |access-date=October 25, 2025}}</ref> It was later divided into five legislative districts from 1907 to 1972.<ref>{{cite PH act |chamber=Act |number=1582 |date=January 9, 1907 |url=https://lawyerly.ph/laws/view/l5054\n |title=An Act to Provide for the Holding of Elections in the Philippine Islands, for the Organization of the Philippine Assembly, and for Other Purposes |access-date=October 25, 2025}}</ref>\n\nIn the disruption caused by the [[Second World War]], two delegates represented the province in the [[National Assembly of the Second Philippine Republic|National Assembly]] of the Japanese-sponsored [[Second Philippine Republic]]: one was the provincial governor (an ''ex officio'' member), while the other was elected through a provincial assembly of [[KALIBAPI]] members during the [[Japanese occupation of the Philippines]].<ref>{{cite web |title=The 1943 Constitution of the Republic of the Philippines |url=https://lawphil.net/consti/cons1943.html\n |website=The LawPhil Project |access-date=October 25, 2025}}</ref> Upon the restoration of the [[Philippine Commonwealth]] in 1945, the province retained its five pre-war representative districts.\n\nIt was part of the representation of [[Interim Batasang Pambansa|Region I]] from 1978 to 1984,<ref>{{cite web |title=Batas Pambansa Blg. 51 \u2013 An Act Providing for the Apportionment of Seats in the Interim Batasang Pambansa and for Other Purposes |url=https://lawphil.net/statutes/bataspam/bp1979/bp_51_1979.html\n |website=The LawPhil Project |date=December 22, 1979 |access-date=October 25, 2025}}</ref> and from 1984 to 1986 it elected [[#1984\u20131986|six assemblymen at-large]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Philippines \u2014 1984 Parliamentary Chamber: Elections held in 1984 |url=https://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/arc/PHILIPPINES_1984_E.PDF\n |website=Inter-Parliamentary Union (IPU) Archives |access-date=October 25, 2025}}</ref>\n\nPangasinan was reapportioned into six congressional districts under the new [[Constitution of the Philippines#1987 Constitution|Constitution]]<ref>{{cite web |author=1986 Constitutional Commission |title=1987 Constitution of the Republic of the Philippines \u2013 Apportionment Ordinance |url=https://www.officialgazette.gov.ph/constitutions/the-1987-constitution-of-the-republic-of-the-philippines/the-1987-constitution-of-the-republic-of-the-philippines-ordinance/\n |date=February 2, 1987 |access-date=October 25, 2025}}</ref> which was proclaimed on February 11, 1987, and elected members to the restored [[House of Representatives of the Philippines|House of Representatives]] starting that same year.\n\n=== Reapportionment Attempts ===\n\n'''2009 Reapportionment Attempt''' \n\nIn May 2009, Representative Victor Agbayani of Pangasinan filed House Bill No. 4140 (14th Congress)|House Bill No. 4140 during the 14th Congress. The bill sought to reapportion the province into seven legislative districts, citing population growth and the need for improved representation. After passing the House of Representatives, the bill was transmitted to the Senate. However, in June 2009, the Senate Committee on Local Government suspended hearings on the measure due to concerns over population data accuracy and potential political implications. As a result, the bill did not progress further and was not enacted into law.<ref>{{cite web |title=House Bill No. 4140, 14th Congress |url=https://ldr.senate.gov.ph/bills/house-bill-no-4140-14th-congress-republic |website=Senate Legislative Documents Repository |access-date=25 October 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Senate body suspends hearing on Pangasinan redistricting |url=https://www.philstar.com/nation/2009/06/08/475187/senate-body-suspends-hearing-pangasinan-redistricting |website=The Philippine Star |date=8 June 2009 |access-date=25 October 2025}}</ref>\n\n'''2022 Reapportionment Attempt'''\n\nIn November 2022, Representative Robert Raymund M. Estrella of Pangasinan filed House Bill No. 6169 during the [[19th Congress of the Philippines|19th Congress]]. The bill proposed to reapportion the province into eight legislative districts, aiming to address population disparities and enhance local governance.<ref>{{cite web |title=House Bill No. 6169, 19th Congress |url=https://ldr.senate.gov.ph/bills/house-bill-no-6169-19th-congress |website=Senate Legislative Documents Repository |access-date=25 October 2025}}</ref>\n\n== Current Districts ==\n{| class=\"wikitable sortable\" style=\"margin: 1em auto; font-size:100%; line-height:20px; text-align:center\"\n|+Legislative districts and representatives of [[Pangasinan]]\n! rowspan=\"2\" |District\n! colspan=\"3\" |Current Representative\n! rowspan=\"2\" |Party\n! rowspan=\"2\" |Constituent [[Local government in the Philippines|LGUs]]\n! rowspan=\"2\" |Population (2024)<ref name=\"PSA-2024IlocosHighlights\">Philippine Statistics Authority. \u201cHighlights of Region I (Ilocos Region) Population 2024 Census of Population (POPCEN).\u201d August 31 2025. https://rsso01.psa.gov.ph/infographics/2024-census-population-ilocos-region-highlights. Accessed [date you accessed the page].</ref>\n\n! rowspan=\"2\" |Area<ref name=\"list\">{{Cite web |title=List of Provinces |url=http://www.nscb.gov.ph/activestats/psgc/listprov.asp |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160419000512/http://www.nscb.gov.ph/activestats/psgc/listprov.asp |archive-date=April 19, 2016 |access-date=August 2, 2022 |website=PSGC Interactive |publisher=National Statistical Coordination Board}}</ref>\n! rowspan=\"2\" |Map\n|-\n! colspan=\"2\" |Image\n!Name\n|-\n![[Pangasinan's 1st congressional district|1st]]\n| style=\"background:{{party color|Nacionalista Party}};\" |\n| [[File:Rep. Arthur Celeste (20th Congress).jpg|150x150px]]\n| style=\"text-align:left;\" |'''[[Arthur Celeste]]'''<br><small>(since [[19th Congress of the Philippines|2022]])</small><br><small>[[Alaminos, Pangasinan|Alaminos]]</small>\n|[[Nacionalista Party|Nacionalista]]\n|{{Collapsible list\n| [[Agno, Pangasinan|Agno]]\n| [[Alaminos, Pangasinan|Alaminos]]\n| [[Anda, Pangasinan|Anda]]\n| [[Bani, Pangasinan|Bani]]\n| [[Bolinao]]\n| [[Burgos, Pangasinan|Burgos]]\n| [[Dasol]]\n| [[Infanta, Pangasinan|Infanta]]\n| [[Mabini, Pangasinan|Mabini]]\n| [[Sual]]\n}}\n| 456,894\n| 1,758.81&nbsp;km<sup>2</sup>\n| [[File:PangasinanDistrict1.svg|150x150px]]\n|-\n![[Pangasinan's 2nd congressional district|2n